        "With both sides going ahead alone, every cut sells about <b>290</b> seats twice. No server failed, nothing errored, p99 stayed about <b>0.24 s</b>: the system stayed up, and it was wrong. That's the choice the CAP theorem names: while the network is cut, a system can stay available or stay consistent, not both.",
        "Needing a majority keeps it right. Two of the three copies live in the east, so the east can still sell; the west, cut off from the majority, has to refuse, about <b>300</b> bookings a cut, and no seat is sold twice. It costs a little even with the link up: every western booking waits for a round trip east, and p99 goes from about 0.24 s to about <b>0.25 s</b>.",
